A||||26||maryjane||possible meteor storm||||1||
Z||000000||maryjane||11-11-2001||08:55 PM||||In the pre-dawn hrs of Nov 18th, the Leonid meteor shower will be at it's best. Predictions vary from 1000-2500/hr in North America to as much as 3500 per hr in Australia. In comparison, 1999 was an average year with 600-900/hr, and only for a short time. NASA has scrubbed a shuttle launch in preparation, satelite damage is possible, space station will be on alert. Look toward the constellation Leo in the upper-Eastern sky from 11pm til an hour before first rays of dawn. Leo resembles a scicle or backwards question mark.<p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 11-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||artherd||11-11-2001||09:22 PM||||Gonna be a good show  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Already have the mountiantop meteor-party spot picked out.  Better start warming the hot chocolate now though.<P>Hey! I have a T-top car this year YES!!!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000002||Ken Wittlief||11-11-2001||11:22 PM||||I read the best time to see it is if you stay up all night saturday, the 17th, until 4AM - the peak will be from 4AM to 6AM (Eastern time zone).<P> technically this is 4AM on Sunday the 18, but somehow from my way of thinking, if you stay up on saturday night until 4AM, to me, its still saturday night.<P> if you wait till sunday nite, the 18th you will have missed it.<P> sounds like it will definately be worth setting the alarm clock for - 1000 per hour is one every 4 seconds - and thats the average - there could be bursts when you will see several at the same time.||||reg||10
Z||000003||maryjane||11-13-2001||01:30 AM||||Thought I'd update this a bit. There are several different predictions of what part of the world will get the best show. All agree it will be the best since 1966, when estimated 100,00 meteors were visible on the W. Coast during a short period. One predictor gave the best times as following:<BR>800 meteors/hr at:<BR>4:55 est<BR>3:55 cst<BR>2:55 mst<BR>1:55 pst<BR> Peter Jenniskins of the SETI Institute and NASA's Ames Research Ctr predicts up to 4200 meteors/hr possible in North America at 5:09 EST. I've not missed any meteor shower in the last 15 yrs and have never seen anything that even approached these numbers, as clear as the skies are here in West Texas. You can check out these links for info & best predictions for a city in your area of the U.S. <A HREF="http://www.space.com/scienceastronomy/astronomy/leonids_2001.html#rates" TARGET=_blank>http://www.space.com/scienceastronomy/astronomy/leonids_2001.html#rates</A> <BR><p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 11-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cheever3000||11-13-2001||09:38 AM||||Every time we have this, the next day the news shows a short video clip, but you can never see anything on it!  Why do they keep trying?  You can't enjoy watching meteors on TV, it's the kind of thing you have to actually go outdoors and experience with your own eyes.<P>I hope it will be the best one since '66 like someone said because the ones I've bothered to watch were disappointing.<BR>||||reg||1
